This contains third party libraries that are uncommon and are therefore shipped
with the Widelands source code for transparent compilation.

Changes
-------

Eris:
- Removed luac.c and lua.c. These sources are used only for stand-alone CLI tools, not usable for game functionality.
- Fixed a MemoryLeak in eris.c in line 2086 when a thread is (un)persisted

Tinygettext:
- Not using the base repository but the fork at `https://github.com/SuperTux/tinygettext`
  (commit 3a90f67ea59e5b792d634ed159526b040b2ce268 2024-02-16 20:24:54 UTC)
- Added ig and krl to list of languages in language.cpp
- CMakeLists: Removed version check

libmd:
- Based of commit d5b8e853989a73f8fff9dc4e00dccd0b691b84f9 (2024-02-21);
  heavily customised to embed it into our portable codebase and avoid name collisions with various system headers and libraries.
- Converted all files to C++, moved them into the "libmd" namespace, and renamed the include guards.
- Included some of the files that are normally generated semi-automatically.
- Removed file input functions specific to POSIX.
- Removed support for MD2, MD4, and RipeMD160.

glad2:
- Generated 2026-08-14 with glad2 2.0.8 (https://github.com/Dav1dde/glad), replacing GLEW/glbinding
  as the renderer's sole GL loader (renderer modernization WP-1, `Claude/RENDERER_MODERNIZATION_PLAN.md`).
- Command: `python -m glad --api "gl:compatibility=3.3,gles2=3.0" --merge --out-path . --reproducible c --debug`
- Desktop GL is generated as **compatibility profile**, not core, even though the eventual core
  backend (WP-4+) only needs core: the still-active legacy GL 2.1 path uploads the dither mask with
  `GL_INTENSITY` (`src/graphic/texture.cc`), a token core-profile headers omit entirely. Compatibility
  is a superset header, safe to compile against regardless of which profile the runtime context ends
  up requesting; only the token's removal (WP-7) requires core-profile behavior, not the header.
- GLES 3.0 is generated in the same merged output because the loader must support it, not because
  anything renders with ES yet (decision 9 of the plan: ES is a constraint, kept reachable, not a
  deliverable). `gladLoadGL()`/`gladLoadGLES2()` share the same `gl*` call surface either way.
- `--debug` generates a per-call pre/post callback wrapper (`gladSetGLPreCallback`/
  `gladSetGLPostCallback`, installed/removed via `gladInstallGLDebug`/`gladUninstallGLDebug`) used to
  implement `--debug_gl_trace`. The wrapper is live by default immediately after `gladLoadGL()` (with
  a default callback that calls `glGetError()` after every GL call) — `gladUninstallGLDebug()` must be
  called explicitly when tracing is not requested to get parity with a non-debug build; once called,
  the `gl*` macros resolve through exactly one function-pointer indirection, identical to a build
  without `--debug`.
- `--reproducible` only suppresses the embedded generation timestamp for a deterministic diff; it does
  not pin the upstream `KhronosGroup/OpenGL-Registry` XML to a commit, so regenerating later may pick
  up newer extensions. All 836 extensions available for the requested API/version pair are included
  (no `--extensions` filter), matching GLEW's behavior of exposing everything the driver reports.
